"VoyagerPublicFacingApps (https://voypubapps.occourts.org/civil-calendar)\nciteturn1239view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view0\",\"id\":316}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVoyagerPublicFacingApps (https://voypubapps.occourts.org/complex-civil-calendar)\nciteturn1239view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view0\",\"id\":317}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndepartment-guidelines.pdf (https://www.occourts.org/system/files/department-guidelines.pdf)\nciteturn1239view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view0\",\"id\":318}); Total lines: 304\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: √ Plaintiff must serve a copy of these Guidelines with the Summons and Complaint.\nL2@P0: GUIDELINES\nL3@P0: ALL COMPLEX CIVIL DEPARTMENTS\nL4@P0: Welcome to the Complex Civil Litigation Program. Orange County Superior Court is\nL5@P0: one of six courts designated by the California Judicial Council as pilot project courts to\nL6@P0: handle solely complex civil litigation. These pilot courts were established to apply case\nL7@P0: management principles to improve the effective administration of justice by reducing the\nL8@P0: time and expense normally associated with the litigation of complex civil cases. It has\nL9@P0: been our experience that these principles make it easier to prepare these cases for trial\nL10@P0: by providing a more orderly framework for the pre-trial phase of the litigation.\nL11@P0: The result is a greater opportunity for early case resolution through mediation and\nL12@P0: settlement, and improving the way complex cases are tried by encouraging the use of\nL13@P0: technology.\nL14@P0: Counsel’s familiarity with the applicable California Rules of Court [“Local Rules”], Local\nL15@P0: Rules – Superior Court of California, County of Orange, and these Guidelines is\nL16@P0: expected. The Guidelines should answer most procedural questions and assist you in\nL17@P0: feeling comfortable in our courtrooms.\nL18@P0: COURTROOM DEMEANOR, CONDUCT AND ETIQUETTE\nL19@P0: Counsel are expected to adhere to the provisions of the California Attorney Guidelines\nL20@P0: of Civility and Professionalism. (State Bar of the State of California, adopted July 20,\nL21@P0-1: 2007, attached to these Guidelines as Appendix 1.)2\nL22@P1: I. GENERAL MATTERS\nL23@P1: 1. When issued by the court, the provisions of the Case Management Order in the\nL24@P1: particular action shall govern over these Guidelines. Procedural matters not\nL25@P1: provided for in these Guidelines or in a Case Management Order shall be\nL26@P1: governed by the pertinent provisions of the California statutes, the California\nL27@P1: Rules of Court, and the California Standards of Judicial Administration. The\nL28@P1: purpose of these Guidelines is to supplement but not contradict the law\nL29@P1: governing civil procedure.\nL30@P1: 2. The Superior Court of California, County of Orange has established a system\nL31@P1: for e-filing in accordance with Code of Civil Procedure §1010.6 and California\nL32@P1: Rules of Court, rule 2.250 et seq. All papers filed in complex civil cases must\nL33@P1: be electronically filed unless a party has been specifically excused by the Court\nL34@P1: from the requirement, pursuant to the Local Rules – Superior Court of\nL35@P1: California, County of Orange (“local rules”), rule 308. To register for the\nL36@P1: program and to obtain additional information, go to:\nL37@P1: www.occourts.org/complexcivil/ .\nL38@P1: 3. Cross-complainants must serve a copy of these guidelines and give notice of\nL39@P1: any scheduled hearings and depositions at the time the cross-complaint is\nL40@P1: served.\nL41@P1: 4. Information about filing requirements or fees is available on the court’s Internet\nL42@P1: home page at: http://www.occourts.org, or by telephone at (657) 622-5314.\nL43@P1: The local rules are available on the court’s public internet home page.\nL44@P1: 5. Telephone appearances are conducted through CourtCall, pursuant to the\nL45@P1: provisions of California Rules of Court, Rule 3.670. Parties are encouraged to\nL46@P1: seek further information concerning guidelines and protocols from CourtCall at\nL47@P1: (310) 342-0888 or (888) 88-COURT.\nL48@P1: II. Initial Case Management Conference:\nL49@P1: The Initial Case Management Conference shall take place in conformance with the\nL50@P1: requirements set forth in California Rules of Court, rule 3.750. The Initial Case\nL51@P1: Management Conference is generally scheduled approximately 90 days after the action\nL52@P1: is filed. Plaintiff is required to give notice of this conference date to all other parties.\nL53@P1: Thereafter, Status Conferences shall be set in consultation with the Court, according to\nL54@P1: the needs of the parties.\nL55@P1: III. Case Management Conference and Status Conference Statements:\nL56@P1: The judges of the Civil Complex Center have determined that Judicial Council form CM\u0002110, Civil Case Management Statement required by California Rules of Court, Rule\nL57@P1: 3.725(c) for some civil cases, is inadequate to provide the judges the information they\nL58@P1-2: need when determining how a particular complex case should be managed. Form CM\u0002110 should not be used in any action designated or provisionally designated as3\nL59@P2: complex. Instead, the parties shall file with the court either a Case Management\nL60@P2: Conference Statement or a Status Conference Statement as described below.\nL61@P2: Counsel must file an updated Conference Statement for each Case Management or\nL62@P2: Status Conference. The Conference Statement is due no later than 5 court days prior\nL63@P2: to the hearing.\nL64@P2: A Status Conference Statement may be filed as an alternative to the Case Management\nL65@P2: Conference Statement when appropriate. A Status Conference Statement is generally\nL66@P2: less detailed than a Case Management Conference Statement and is to be used to\nL67@P2: advise the court of progress or developments in the case which have occurred since the\nL68@P2: last review hearing.\nL69@P2: A joint statement of the parties is preferred by the court whenever possible.\nL70@P2: IV. CASE MANAGEMENT ORDERS:\nL71@P2: Case Management Orders are not required in all cases, but they may be helpful in\nL72@P2: cases where the sequencing and timing of key events is necessary in the management\nL73@P2: of the litigation and preparation of the case for trial. However, even if a Case\nL74@P2: Management Order is not necessary in a particular case, all complex cases must be\nL75@P2: managed by counsel, or the court, or both.\nL76@P2: The goal of case management is to bring about a just resolution as speedily and\nL77@P2: economically as possible. To be effective, case management should be tailored to the\nL78@P2: needs of the particular litigation and to the resources available; make-work activity\nL79@P2: should be avoided. The parties or the court should develop and monitor an effective\nL80@P2: plan for the orderly conduct of pretrial and trial proceedings. A case management plan\nL81@P2: should prescribe a series of procedural steps, with firm dates, giving direction and order\nL82@P2: to the case as it progresses through pretrial proceedings to summary disposition or trial.\nL83@P2: The setting of interim time limits and deadlines is often a necessary part of an effective\nL84@P2: case management plan.\nL85@P2: V. LAW AND MOTION:\nL86@P2: 1. Meet and Confer: This court adopts the view that pre-filing conferences\nL87@P2: between counsel may be useful in avoiding useless or unnecessary motions.\nL88@P2: Therefore, prior to the hearing of any motion, petition or application, except\nL89@P2: applications to appear pro hac vice and motions to withdraw as counsel of\nL90@P2: record, all counsel and parties appearing in propria persona shall confer in a\nL91@P2: good faith attempt to eliminate the necessity of the hearing or resolve as many\nL92@P2: disputes as possible.\nL93@P2: Counsel for the moving party shall arrange the conference to meet and confer\nL94@P2: and, at least 3 calendar days before the hearing, file with the court a statement\nL95@P2: entitled “Meet and Confer,” summarizing the issues remaining in dispute and\nL96@P2-3: the respective positions taken.4\nL97@P3: 2. Tentative Rulings: Members of the Complex Civil Panel may publish tentative\nL98@P3: law and motion rulings by any system described in Local Rule 382.\nL99@P3: 3. Off Calendars and Continuances: In order to promote judicial economy and\nL100@P3: avoid wasting court resources, counsel for moving parties must notify the\nL101@P3: courtroom clerk as soon as possible if any matter will be taken off calendar.\nL102@P3: Stipulations between the parties to continue a matter must be approved by the\nL103@P3: court.\nL104@P3: VI. EX PARTE APPLICATIONS:\nL105@P3: 1. The court’s consideration of an ex parte application will not interfere with or\nL106@P3: delay any trial in progress. The moving party is expected to adhere to the\nL107@P3: provisions of California Rules of Court, Rule 3.1200 – 3.1207. All papers\nL108@P3: necessary to the determination of the application, including any proposed\nL109@P3: pleading, motion or order, must be submitted with the ex parte application.\nL110@P3: Counsel should contact the courtroom clerk to verify any specific deadlines for\nL111@P3: the submission of moving papers or other preferences applicable to that\nL112@P3: department. Counsel may also contact the courtroom clerk to inquire if oral\nL113@P3: argument will be permitted, or if the court will rule based on the application and\nL114@P3: any written opposition.\nL115@P3: 2. The application shall include a declaration of Notice of Ex Parte Hearing and a\nL116@P3: proposed order; and shall state in the notice the irreparable harm, immediate\nL117@P3: danger or other basis for ex parte relief that will result if the requested relief is\nL118@P3: not granted until a regularly noticed motion may be heard.\nL119@P3: VII. MANDATORY SETTLEMENT CONFERENCES (“MSC’S”):\nL120@P3: Compliance with Local Rule 316 is required.\nL121@P3: All of the judges at the Civil Complex Center are willing to help another judge in the\nL122@P3: settlement of a complex case depending upon the judge’s available calendar. If the\nL123@P3: parties agree to have a mandatory settlement conference conducted by a judge other\nL124@P3: than the assigned judge, the parties should first determine the other judge’s availability\nL125@P3: before asking the assigned judge to order the settlement conference. However, it is not\nL126@P3: presumed that the judge to whom a case is assigned should not conduct the mandatory\nL127@P3: settlement conferences in his or her cases. If a party objects to the trial judge’s\nL128@P3: participation in the MSC, the party must advise the judge or the courtroom clerk of its\nL129@P3: objection prior to the setting of the MSC. Counsel are advised to check with the court to\nL130@P3: determine its preference in this regard.\nL131@P3: VIII. Pre-trial Conferences\nL132@P3: 1. A Pre-trial Conference may be scheduled 30-90 days before trial for the\nL133@P3: purpose of determining the readiness of the parties and resolving procedural\nL134@P3: issues concerning the trial. The goal of the Pre-trial Conference is to make the\nL135@P3-4: trial proceed as predictably and smoothly as possible. The Pre-trial5\nL136@P4: Conference is not a substitute for the Issues Conference required by\nL137@P4: Local Rule 317.\nL138@P4: 2. At the Pre-trial Conference, counsel should be prepared to state whether his or\nL139@P4: her client will be using the electronic presentation of evidence at the trial. Using\nL140@P4: electronic equipment to present evidence at trial requires preparation,\nL141@P4: organization and cooperation by the parties. The court expects that the parties\nL142@P4: will work together in devising a protocol for the pre-marking of exhibits by using\nL143@P4: prefixes or a super-numeration system to designate the proponent of the\nL144@P4: evidence. Where there are multiple pages to a single exhibit, each page should\nL145@P4: be bates-stamped. Counsel should contact the courtroom clerk to determine if\nL146@P4: the trial judge has a specific preference for how exhibits should be marked.\nL147@P4: 3. In a case where it is reasonable to presume voluminous documents will be\nL148@P4: produced during discovery, counsel are urged to agree upon a protocol for the\nL149@P4: pre-marking of exhibits at the earliest time possible, preferably before the\nL150@P4: initiation of discovery and delivery to a document depository. It is less\nL151@P4: expensive to mark and index voluminous documents as they are deposited\nL152@P4: than when it is done on the eve of trial.\nL153@P4: 4. Counsel are required to cooperate throughout the trial so that one party’s\nL154@P4: electronic exhibits are available to the other side to display during cross\u0002examination.\nL155@P4: 5. The electronic version of documents, photographs, charts or other\nL156@P4: demonstrative evidence may be substituted for the actual exhibit at trial upon\nL157@P4: the stipulation of the parties and order of the court. This guideline is not meant\nL158@P4: to alter the rules of discovery or the obligation of a party to make available the\nL159@P4: original of a document for inspection by another party through discovery or at\nL160@P4: the Issues Conference.\nL161@P4: 6. Physical exhibits and documents are not required to be presented in a\nL162@P4: digitalized format. However, evidence which has not been presented in\nL163@P4: electronic form customarily will be ordered by the court returned at the end of\nL164@P4: the appeal period to the party which offered it. Before trial commences, counsel\nL165@P4: will be asked to sign a stipulation for the return and maintenance of the exhibits.\nL166@P4: Plaintiff will maintain joint exhibits unless the court orders otherwise.\nL167@P4: IX. Use of the Court’s Evidence Presentation Systems\nL168@P4: 1. On-Site Electronic Evidence Presentation Systems: Every courtroom has\nL169@P4: the capability of being equipped with court-based evidence presentation\nL170@P4: systems for use by the parties. Counsel are strongly encouraged to take\nL171@P4: advantage of the benefits of the electronic presentation of evidence when in\nL172@P4: trial at the Civil Complex Center to enhance the orderly and effective\nL173@P4: presentation of evidence, reduce concerns about the custody and security of\nL174@P4: exhibits, and reduce the work and expense associated with the tagging, storing\nL175@P4-5: and transporting of exhibits. In an appropriate case, the court may require the6\nL176@P5: use of an electronic evidence presentation system. Electronic evidence\nL177@P5: presentation systems must be compatible with the court’s infrastructure (video\nL178@P5: distribution amplifier, wiring, conduit, floor receptacles and connectors).\nL179@P5: 2. Electronic Evidence Standard Format: Counsel presenting evidence that is\nL180@P5: exclusively electronic in form must present the evidence in PDF file format and\nL181@P5: stored on CD-R. Whenever evidence is presented electronically, the physical\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProbate Courtroom Schedule & Requirements | Superior Court of California | County of Orange (https://www.occourts.org/divisions/probate/probate-courtroom-schedule-requirements)\nciteturn1239view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view0\",\"id\":220}); Total lines: 539\n"
